1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,775
The Yokohama Specie Bank:
Japan's Bank in Prewar Singapore

2
00:00:04,775 --> 00:00:10,009
On the 20th of March 1942, five
weeks after the British surrender,

3
00:00:10,089 --> 00:00:12,666
the Yokohama Specie Bank reopened

4
00:00:12,746 --> 00:00:16,450
for business in Singapore,
now renamed Syonan.

5
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:20,650
It did not go back to its
own office in Meyer Chambers.

6
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:24,542
A notice in the Syonan Shimbun
gave its new address as the

7
00:00:24,622 --> 00:00:27,488
"Late Hongkong and
Shanghai Banking Corp.",

8
00:00:27,568 --> 00:00:32,225
the building of the British bank
whose staff had just been interned.

9
00:00:32,225 --> 00:00:36,347
The Yokohama Specie Bank had
been in Singapore since 1916,

10
00:00:36,427 --> 00:00:40,187
financing the colony's trade
with Japan from a corner

11
00:00:40,267 --> 00:00:43,810
of Raffles Place, and for
most of that time it was

12
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:47,875
simply one of the foreign
exchange banks in the square.

13
00:00:47,875 --> 00:00:50,746
Its story shows how closely a bank could

14
00:00:50,826 --> 00:00:53,925
be tied to the fortunes of its government.

15
00:00:53,925 --> 00:00:58,832
The Yokohama Specie Bank was
founded in Yokohama in 1880 and

16
00:00:58,912 --> 00:01:03,900
opened offices in New York that
year and in London the next.

17
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:07,042
Its name came from specie, the silver coin

18
00:01:07,122 --> 00:01:10,575
then used to settle
payments between traders.

19
00:01:10,575 --> 00:01:14,817
It was a quasi-governmental
bank that acted as the Japanese

20
00:01:14,897 --> 00:01:20,311
government's financial agent abroad,
and its business was foreign exchange:

21
00:01:20,391 --> 00:01:23,175
financing Japan's imports and exports.

22
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:26,915
By the 1920s it handled nearly half of the

23
00:01:26,995 --> 00:01:31,462
foreign exchange for Japan's
trade, and by 1929 it

24
00:01:31,542 --> 00:01:35,725
had 37 offices outside
Japan and its colonies.

25
00:01:35,725 --> 00:01:40,442
The Straits Times announced the
bank's arrival in a single sentence

26
00:01:40,522 --> 00:01:45,383
on the 5th of September 1916: "An
agency of the Yokohama Specie Bank,

27
00:01:45,463 --> 00:01:50,753
Limited, has been established in
Singapore and will start business at 31-a,

28
00:01:50,833 --> 00:01:54,700
Kling Street, and 6-a, Bonham
Street, as from to-day."

29
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:57,320
The address was the Bonham Building,

30
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:00,025
at the corner facing Raffles Place.

31
00:02:00,025 --> 00:02:03,827
Kling Street was renamed
Chulia Street in 1922,

32
00:02:03,907 --> 00:02:07,875
and in 1935 the same
address, 31-A Chulia Street,

33
00:02:07,955 --> 00:02:12,088
became the first office of
the United Chinese Bank,

34
00:02:12,168 --> 00:02:17,950
today's United Overseas Bank, which
is the subject of an earlier post.

35
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:22,025
The bank arrived in the
middle of the First World War.

36
00:02:22,025 --> 00:02:25,777
Its Singapore business was
the same as everywhere else:

37
00:02:25,857 --> 00:02:29,539
financing trade with Japan
for the Japanese merchants,

38
00:02:29,619 --> 00:02:34,138
shipping lines and companies in
the colony, alongside the British,

39
00:02:34,218 --> 00:02:38,050
Dutch and American exchange
banks around Raffles Place.

40
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:41,528
The branch's managers were
posted from the bank's

41
00:02:41,608 --> 00:02:44,875
other offices and moved
on after a few years.

42
00:02:44,875 --> 00:02:49,665
In 1927 the bank sent Mr
Nakamura, the sub-manager of its

43
00:02:49,745 --> 00:02:54,364
New York branch, to replace
Jukichi Okada, who had been

44
00:02:54,444 --> 00:02:59,400
transferred to Surabaya, and
in 1930 H. Unagami took over.

45
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:03,040
In September 1931 Unagami
spoke to the Rotary

46
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:06,925
Club of Singapore about
Japan's economic crisis

47
00:03:07,005 --> 00:03:10,725
and its efforts to stay
on the gold standard.

48
00:03:10,725 --> 00:03:15,726
On the 3rd of April 1933 the bank
moved out of the Bonham Building

49
00:03:15,806 --> 00:03:20,268
to Meyer Chambers, a new
five-storey building at the corner

50
00:03:20,348 --> 00:03:25,275
of Raffles Place and Malacca
Street, completed in December 1930.

51
00:03:25,275 --> 00:03:30,303
Its advertisements in the 1930s
gave its capital as 100 million yen,

52
00:03:30,383 --> 00:03:33,232
fully paid up, and listed branches from

53
00:03:33,312 --> 00:03:37,438
Alexandria and Bombay to
Hamburg, Los Angeles and Paris,

54
00:03:37,518 --> 00:03:42,100
with the Singapore branch at
"Meyer Chambers, Raffles Place."

55
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:47,225
In July 1941 Britain
froze Japanese assets.

56
00:03:47,225 --> 00:03:50,790
In August the Straits
Times reported that only "a

57
00:03:50,870 --> 00:03:54,584
few thousand sterling" of
the frozen funds would be

58
00:03:54,664 --> 00:03:58,526
available to the Japanese
for trade, against a normal

59
00:03:58,606 --> 00:04:02,400
peacetime Anglo-Japanese
trade of about £7 million.

60
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,261
On the 8th of December
1941, as Japanese forces

61
00:04:06,341 --> 00:04:10,035
landed in Malaya, the
colonial authorities in

62
00:04:10,115 --> 00:04:14,475
Singapore arrested Japanese
residents of the colony.

63
00:04:14,475 --> 00:04:18,995
According to a 2021 study by the
historians Christine de Matos

64
00:04:19,075 --> 00:04:23,743
and Rowena Ward, about 2,700
civilians from across British South

65
00:04:23,823 --> 00:04:28,195
and South-East Asia, most of
them from Singapore and Malaya,

66
00:04:28,275 --> 00:04:32,720
were shipped to internment camps
in India, at the Purana Qila

67
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:37,400
in New Delhi and later at Deoli,
where many stayed until 1946.

68
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,500
Singapore surrendered on
the 15th of February 1942.

69
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:47,177
A month later, on the 17th of
March, the Syonan Shimbun announced

70
00:04:47,257 --> 00:04:52,739
that the Yokohama Specie Bank and the
Bank of Taiwan, another Japanese bank,

71
00:04:52,819 --> 00:04:57,131
would reopen on the 20th of
March "at their former buildings

72
00:04:57,211 --> 00:05:01,675
in Raffles Place", working from
10 a.m. to 3 p.m. Tokyo time.

73
00:05:01,675 --> 00:05:05,425
On the day itself, a
notice changed the plan.

74
00:05:05,425 --> 00:05:09,470
The Yokohama Specie Bank would
be at the former Hongkong and

75
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:14,076
Shanghai Banking Corporation, "not
at Meyer Chambers as announced",

76
00:05:14,156 --> 00:05:18,625
and the Bank of Taiwan at the
former Netherlands Trading Society.

77
00:05:18,625 --> 00:05:22,367
By early 1943 the bank's
advertisements gave its address

78
00:05:22,447 --> 00:05:25,984
as 21 Collyer Quay, Syonan,
with S. Mutoh as manager,

79
00:05:26,064 --> 00:05:30,625
and listed its "branches in the
Southern Regions": twelve in Malaya,

80
00:05:30,705 --> 00:05:34,379
from Penang and Ipoh to Kota
Bahru and Kuala Trengganu,

81
00:05:34,459 --> 00:05:37,928
ten in Sumatra, six in
Borneo, and offices in Burma,

82
00:05:38,008 --> 00:05:41,625
Java, the Philippines,
Thailand and French Indochina.

83
00:05:41,625 --> 00:05:45,617
In November 1942 the military
administration named it as

84
00:05:45,697 --> 00:05:49,253
one of four banks handling
the exchange of Malayan

85
00:05:49,333 --> 00:05:54,779
dollars and Dutch guilders, alongside
the Southern Regions Development Bank,

86
00:05:54,859 --> 00:05:57,550
which issued the occupation currency.

87
00:05:57,550 --> 00:06:02,675
That currency, the "banana money",
is the subject of another post.

88
00:06:02,675 --> 00:06:07,725
The bank also handled the money
left behind by the British banks.

89
00:06:07,725 --> 00:06:12,403
In September 1944 the military
administration's notice No.

90
00:06:12,483 --> 00:06:17,818
52 ordered a partial repayment to
depositors of the "enemy banks",

91
00:06:17,898 --> 00:06:22,000
provided they were not
themselves enemy nationals.

92
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,618
The Yokohama Specie Bank
was to pay the depositors of

93
00:06:25,698 --> 00:06:29,177
the Hongkong and Shanghai
Bank, the Chartered Bank,

94
00:06:29,257 --> 00:06:33,025
the Mercantile Bank, Thomas
Cook and American Express.

95
00:06:33,025 --> 00:06:36,690
Deposits below $100 would be paid in full,

96
00:06:36,770 --> 00:06:40,346
and larger deposits would be paid 100 per

97
00:06:40,426 --> 00:06:44,350
cent up to $100 and 20
per cent of the rest.

98
00:06:44,350 --> 00:06:47,561
The chart shows where the
bank's Singapore office

99
00:06:47,641 --> 00:06:51,200
was in each period, and when
its successor came back.

100
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:55,966
After Japan's surrender in 1945,
the Yokohama Specie Bank was

101
00:06:56,046 --> 00:07:01,289
closed by the American occupation
authorities in Japan, and in July

102
00:07:01,369 --> 00:07:06,612
1947 the Singapore Free Press
reported that the Japanese government

103
00:07:06,692 --> 00:07:11,300
had designated it a "closed
institution" to be liquidated.

104
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:15,500
Its business passed to a
new bank, the Bank of Tokyo.

105
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,890
It took more than a decade for a

106
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:20,825
Japanese bank to return to Singapore.

107
00:07:20,825 --> 00:07:24,235
In March 1955 the Bank of
Tokyo's representative,

108
00:07:24,315 --> 00:07:28,580
Yasuo Ishii, had been in the
colony for three months studying

109
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:32,212
whether a branch could be
opened, and the Singapore

110
00:07:32,292 --> 00:07:35,925
Free Press reported that
there were "difficulties".

111
00:07:35,925 --> 00:07:39,774
In August 1956 the Straits
Times reported that the

112
00:07:39,854 --> 00:07:43,702
bank hoped to recruit some
of its staff from local

113
00:07:43,782 --> 00:07:48,025
employees of the Yokohama
Specie Bank's prewar branch.

114
00:07:48,025 --> 00:07:53,067
The branch opened in Phillip
Street on the 19th of January 1957,

115
00:07:53,147 --> 00:07:56,429
after two and a half years of negotiation,

116
00:07:56,509 --> 00:07:59,950
with more than 500
guests at the reception.

117
00:07:59,950 --> 00:08:03,893
The Straits Times noted that
it was the first Japanese

118
00:08:03,973 --> 00:08:07,400
bank to reopen in Malaya
since the Liberation.

119
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:11,803
The Bank of Tokyo later merged
into what is now MUFG Bank,

120
00:08:11,883 --> 00:08:17,525
which traces its international business
back to the Yokohama Specie Bank.

121
00:08:17,525 --> 00:08:22,936
Japanese banks are again a familiar
part of Singapore's financial centre,

122
00:08:23,016 --> 00:08:26,175
alongside banks from many other countries.

123
00:08:26,175 --> 00:08:30,386
Before the war the Yokohama
Specie Bank was an exchange bank

124
00:08:30,466 --> 00:08:34,461
serving its country's trade,
and during the Occupation it

125
00:08:34,541 --> 00:08:38,975
became part of the military
administration's financial system.

126
00:08:38,975 --> 00:08:43,958
Today a foreign bank in Singapore
is licensed and supervised by the

127
00:08:44,038 --> 00:08:48,875
Monetary Authority of Singapore,
whatever its country of origin.

128
00:08:48,875 --> 00:08:52,413
Where it fits in the bigger
story: For a quarter of a

129
00:08:52,493 --> 00:08:56,167
century the Yokohama Specie
Bank was one of the foreign

130
00:08:56,247 --> 00:09:00,808
banks of Raffles Place, a few doors
from the British exchange banks,

131
00:09:00,888 --> 00:09:03,550
financing Singapore's trade with Japan.

132
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:08,488
Its story follows the arc of Japan's
relationship with the colony:

133
00:09:08,568 --> 00:09:12,213
it arrived with the trade
of the First World War,

134
00:09:12,293 --> 00:09:17,079
was frozen out in 1941, ran the
British banks' leftover deposits

135
00:09:17,159 --> 00:09:22,025
during the Occupation, and came
back in 1957 under another name.
